/**
  @page guides-solvers Solvers
  @brief Choose a solver from the assembled system, then verify its result.

  A solver acts on the algebraic system produced by the weak formulation and
  its constraints. Solver selection depends on matrix properties, including nullspaces, boundary
  constraints, coupling and complex coefficients.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-solvers-choosing Solver selection

  | Assembled system | Starting point | Required check |
  | :--- | :--- | :--- |
  | Symmetric/Hermitian positive definite | @ref Rodin::Solver::CG "CG" | Constraints remove the kernel; preconditioning is compatible |
  | Nonsymmetric | @ref Rodin::Solver::GMRES "GMRES" or another suitable Krylov method | Convergence and preconditioner quality |
  | Nonsingular sparse, modest factorization cost | @ref Rodin::Solver::SparseLU "SparseLU" | Factorization status, residual, and memory use |
  | Mixed saddle-point system | Small direct solve or a block-preconditioned method | Stable spaces and a valid gauge/nullspace treatment |
  | Nonlinear residual | Newton-type method with an inner linear solver | Consistent tangent, state updates, and nonlinear convergence |

  The @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Dirichlet Poisson" and clamped
  @ref examples-pdes-elasticity "elasticity" examples use CG. The
  @ref examples-pdes-helmholtz "Helmholtz" matrix is indefinite, while
  @ref examples-pdes-stokes "Stokes" has coupled velocity–pressure blocks.
  These systems require solvers compatible with their indefinite or saddle-point
  structure.

  A periodic or pure-Neumann Poisson problem requires a compatible load and a
  normalization that removes its constant nullspace before a nonsingular algebraic
  solve.

  @section guides-solvers-iterative Iterative convergence

  In the unit-square Poisson program, `poisson` is a defined `Problem` object.

  @snippet PDEs/Poisson.cpp poisson-solve

  The tolerance specifies the backend's algebraic stopping criterion.
  `setMaxIterations(...)` bounds the iteration count. Check the termination status
  before exporting the field, and assess discretization error through mesh
  refinement.

  @subsection guides-solvers-iterative-cg Algebraic diagnostics

  Native CG and GMRES expose `success()`, `getIterationNumber()`, and
  `getError()`. The error value is a backend convergence estimate. An explicit
  residual supplies a separate algebraic check.

  @code{.cpp}
  const auto& system = poisson.getLinearSystem();
  const Real relativeResidual =
    (system.getOperator() * system.getSolution() - system.getVector()).norm()
    / system.getVector().norm();
  @endcode

  This relative definition assumes a nonzero load vector. For a zero load,
  report the absolute residual or state another normalization explicitly.
  Conditioning relates the algebraic residual to solution error. Mesh refinement is
  used to assess discretization convergence.

  @subsection guides-solvers-gmres GMRES configuration

  For a defined native sparse problem, GMRES can be configured as follows.

  @code{.cpp}
  Solver::GMRES solver(problem);
  solver.setTolerance(1e-10).setMaxIterations(1000).setRestart(40).solve();
  if (!solver.success())
    return EXIT_FAILURE;
  @endcode

  Include `<cstdlib>` when using `EXIT_FAILURE`. The restart dimension bounds
  the retained Krylov basis; a smaller value reduces basis storage but can slow
  or prevent convergence. The native default preconditioner is diagonal.
  A compatible Eigen preconditioner can be supplied as a constructor argument;
  the public solver type remains parameterized by the linear-system type.

  For incompressible flow, the preconditioner must account for the coupled velocity
  and pressure blocks and their gauge or nullspace treatment.

  @section guides-solvers-direct Direct-solver cost

  Sparse LU factors a matrix and solves using those factors. It is useful for
  small verification problems and the tutorial-scale
  @ref examples-pdes-helmholtz "Helmholtz" and
  @ref examples-pdes-stokes "Stokes" solves. A nonsingular matrix is a necessary
  mathematical condition; numerical stability and successful factorization
  must still be checked.

  Factorization creates fill-in. Memory and time depend on ordering, graph structure, dimension, polynomial degree
  and conditioning. Factorization cost should be measured for the chosen
  discretization.
  A three-dimensional mixed system can exhaust memory well before a similarly
  sized two-dimensional scalar system.

  @ref Rodin::Solver::SimplicialLLT "SimplicialLLT" supplies a sparse Cholesky
  alternative for suitable positive definite systems. Other direct wrappers
  and optional SuiteSparse integrations are listed in the
  @ref Rodin::Solver "solver reference". Available dependencies and backend
  specializations must be checked for the configured build.

  @section guides-solvers-usage Object lifetimes

  A configured solver is useful when its status must be checked or its settings
  adjusted. A temporary `Solver::CG(problem).solve()` also solves a problem,
  but discards the solver object and its diagnostics immediately.

  Reassigning a problem body marks its assembly stale. If existing coefficient
  data change, explicitly reassemble or rebuild as required by the
  @ref guides-numerical-contracts "numerical contracts". Factorization reuse follows the contract of the selected backend and API.

  Changing the mesh or finite element space changes the algebraic layout.
  Construct a new problem, linear system and associated solvers for the new layout.

  @section guides-solvers-nonlinear Nonlinear residuals

  For a residual @f$F(x)=0@f$, Newton's method solves
  @f[
    J(x^k)\delta x^k=-F(x^k),\qquad
    x^{k+1}=x^k+\alpha\delta x^k.
  @f]
  The nonlinear formulation supplies the state @f$x^k@f$, residual and consistent
  tangent @f$J(x^k)@f$. The Newton solver computes an increment and applies the
  damping @f$\alpha@f$.

  @ref Rodin::Solver::NewtonSolver "NewtonSolver" wraps an associated linear
  solver and updates the supplied state. Its residual stopping criteria are
  @f$\|F(x^k)\|\le\mathrm{atol}@f$ or
  @f$\|F(x^k)\|\le\mathrm{rtol}\|F(x^0)\|@f$.
  An optional step tolerance is disabled by default. After solving, `converged()`
  and `getReport()` expose the termination result; a converged inner linear
  solve alone is insufficient.

  The @ref guides-solid-mechanics "solid mechanics guide" develops the current
  internal virtual-work residual and consistent tangent. The @ref examples-pdes-navierstokes "native cavity tutorial" uses one semi-implicit Oseen solve per time step.

  @section guides-solvers-petsc PETSc configuration

  @ref Rodin::Solver::KSP "KSP" configures PETSc linear systems, and
  @ref Rodin::Solver::SNES "SNES" supplies nonlinear solves using an associated
  KSP. PETSc-backed trial and test functions select PETSc algebra when that backend is
  enabled in the build.

  For a PETSc program that initializes PETSc, processes its solver options,
  and defines the associated problem, command-line options can select the
  method and diagnostics.

  @code{.sh}
  ./program -ksp_type gmres -pc_type ilu -ksp_rtol 1e-8 -ksp_monitor
  @endcode

  These options configure GMRES with ILU for a compatible sequential matrix. MPI ownership, field splits, pressure nullspaces,
  and distributed preconditioner support require the
  @ref guides-petsc "PETSc guide". The reference pages specify the distinct
  native and PETSc convergence/status interfaces.

  @section guides-solvers-troubleshooting Solve diagnostics

  | Observation | Checks before changing algorithms |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | CG fails or stagnates | Symmetry, definiteness, unresolved modes, scaling, and preconditioner |
  | Sparse factorization fails | Gauge, boundary constraints, conditioning, and factorization diagnostic |
  | Factorization exhausts memory | Fill-in, ordering, memory measurements, and a suitable iterative/block method |
  | Iterative solve reaches its limit | True residual, scaling, preconditioner, restart dimension, and tolerance |
  | Small residual but an inaccurate field | Formulation, loads, mesh, quadrature, boundary data, and conditioning |
  | Nonlinear solve fails | State synchronization, residual/tangent consistency, constraints, initial guess, and damping |

  @section guides-solvers-example Examples

  The @ref examples-pdes-poisson "Poisson", @ref examples-pdes-helmholtz "Helmholtz",
  and @ref examples-pdes-stokes "Stokes" tutorials include complete source and
  output. The @ref guides-variational "variational guide" explains assembly,
  while @ref guides-numerical-contracts "numerical contracts" records the
  shared signs, quadrature, and lifetime rules.
*/
